<template>
    <SiteShell page="features">
        <div class="page-head">
            <h1>Features</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                Three lines share one deck model: playback, visual editing and
                agent authoring. Nothing here is assumed from a file extension.
            </p>
        </div>

        <section class="section">
            <h2>Playback <span class="tag">static first</span></h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Open a deck and go full screen on the current display.</li>
                <li>Page forward and back with the keyboard or mouse.</li>
                <li>Hidden slides are skipped in the show but stay editable.</li>
                <li>Speaker notes travel with each page.</li>
                <li>Builds, media sync and transitions are added after the static loop.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <h2>Editing</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Pages, masters and layouts keep stable identities.</li>
                <li>Text, images, shapes, tables, charts and formulas are native objects.</li>
                <li>Edits run in transactions: a failed change leaves no half page behind.</li>
                <li>Undo and redo step through revisions; save and reopen preserve them.</li>
                <li>Concurrent edits are rejected with a revision conflict instead of silently overwriting.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <h2>Agent authoring</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Brief the agent with audience, purpose, duration, material and brand rules.</li>
                <li>The agent plans the argument and the page order before drawing anything.</li>
                <li>It picks a theme and semantic layouts, then fills native objects.</li>
                <li>Rule checks report overflow, contrast, alignment and missing assets with locations.</li>
                <li>Review happens per page; revisions target an object id and a revision.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <h2>Format honesty</h2>
            <p>
                PPTX is the first exchange format, and it is never described as
                “supported” just because a package opens. Each object family,
                animation and style is classified as supported, partial or
                unsupported, with the loss report attached. The legacy binary
                <code>.ppt</code> format is a separate capability.
            </p>
        </section>

